            <ArticleTitle>Ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency presenting as recurrent abdominal pain in childhood.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>850-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/PEC.0b013e31822c25c9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recurrent abdominal pain remains one of the most common symptoms in pediatrics. We present the case of a 3-year-old girl who had recurrent episodes of abdominal pain requiring more than 13 visits to the emergency department. A diagnosis of ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency was eventually made. Urea cycle disorders often present beyond the neonatal period with frequent vomiting episodes; however, recurrent abdominal pain as a presenting symptom is unusual. Unnecessary invasive investigations of recurrent abdominal pain in childhood can be avoided by considering inborn errors of metabolism earlier in the differential diagnosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
